From aba0f922b in plasma/breeze (Plasma/6.7 branch)
Subject: [PATCH 4] SE_ItemViewItemText: take the horizontal inset only from
 space the text does not need, in every case

Supersedes patch 0003, which is retained because it was measured and its
regression is part of the record. 0003 carried two exclusions — word-wrapped
text and Qt::ElideNone — for which it kept the full inset. Both turned out to be
the worse trade, for the same reason: in both cases the text still loses those
pixels, it is merely lost by a different mechanism.

What 0003 cost, measured
------------------------
KeePassXC's entry-editor sidebar (`CategoryListWidget`) is a QListView in
IconMode with wordWrap enabled (`CategoryListWidget.ui`, viewMode and wordWrap
properties). QAbstractItemView::viewOptions() therefore sets
QStyleOptionViewItem::WrapText, 0003's exclusion fired, the full inset was kept,
and every label in a 96px sidebar was elided:

  text          adv  natural  textW  inset  drawn   result
  Entry          32       38     32      6     26   elided to "En..."
  Icon           26       32     26      6     20   elided to "I..."
  Auto-Type      61       67     61      6     55   elided to "Auto-..."

Patch 0001 does not elide these, because it removes the inset unconditionally.
0003 was therefore a regression against 0001 in this layout — it fixed less than
0001 did. Fusion renders the same sidebar in full, confirming the style is the
variable.

This patch removes both exclusions. With the guard applied unconditionally the
same sidebar gives inset=0, drawn=32 for "Entry" against an advance of 32, and
the label fits.

Why removing them is the better trade
-------------------------------------
For word-wrapped text, horizontalAdvance() of the whole string is an
overestimate: the text may occupy several shorter lines. The guard therefore
releases more of the inset than strictly necessary. The cost is clearance that
was not needed rather than a character, because the amount released is bounded
by the inset and is only released when the string does not fit on one line.

For Qt::ElideNone the text is clipped rather than elided, but it is lost either
way, so preserving clearance at its expense is the worse trade.

Across the tested matrix this patch is never worse than 0001, while still
preserving the inset wherever the item has width to spare.

Applies to Qt5 and Qt6 alike; no version conditional is introduced.

Scope of the claim
------------------
The guard is a heuristic validated against the measured matrix, not a proof of
general correctness. It uses QFontMetrics::horizontalAdvance() as its estimate
of the width the string needs, while Qt lays the string out through QTextLayout.
Where the advance overestimates — wrapped and multi-line text, and shaping that
narrows a run — the guard releases more of the inset than required, costing
clearance and nothing else. Were it to underestimate, the guard would retain
more of the inset than the string can spare, and the result would be worse than
0001 rather than better. No such case appeared in the 3888 configurations
measured, but that matrix does not cover tree or table views, SelectRows
selection behaviour, RTL layouts, explicit line breaks, complex shaping, or
non-default fonts and display scaling.

This is the trade the patch makes deliberately: it keeps the clearance
aba0f922b was added to provide, at the cost of depending on a width estimate.
Patches 0001-0003 are hypothesis tests retained as the experimental record, not
alternatives being proposed; 0004 is the proposed change.

--- a/kstyle/breezestyle.cpp
+++ b/kstyle/breezestyle.cpp
@@ -1062,8 +1062,39 @@
         QRect rect = ParentStyleClass::subElementRect(element, option, widget);
         if (viewItem) {
             const QMargins margins = _helper->itemViewItemMargins(viewItem);
-            rect.setRight(rect.right() - margins.right() - Metrics::ItemView_ItemPaddingWidth);
-            rect.setLeft(rect.left() + margins.left() + Metrics::ItemView_ItemPaddingWidth);
+            const int leftInset = margins.left() + Metrics::ItemView_ItemPaddingWidth;
+            const int rightInset = margins.right() + Metrics::ItemView_ItemPaddingWidth;
+
+            // Take the inset only out of space the text does not need. Where the
+            // item is wide enough to spare it, the full inset is applied exactly
+            // as before; where it is not, the text keeps the width it needs
+            // rather than being elided by the inset itself.
+            //
+            // The comparison is made against the width the text is really laid
+            // out in, not against rect.width(): QCommonStylePrivate::
+            // viewItemDrawText() removes PM_FocusFrameHMargin + 1 from each side
+            // again, after this function has returned.
+            //
+            // No case is excluded, deliberately. For word-wrapped text
+            // horizontalAdvance() of the whole string overestimates what is
+            // required, so the guard releases more of the inset than strictly
+            // necessary; that costs clearance which was not needed rather than
+            // a character. Excluding wrapped text and keeping the full inset
+            // instead elides labels in icon-mode views, KeePassXC's entry
+            // editor sidebar among them. The same reasoning applies to
+            // Qt::ElideNone, where the text is clipped rather than elided but
+            // is lost either way.
+            const int textMargin = proxy()->pixelMetric(PM_FocusFrameHMargin, option, widget) + 1;
+            const int available = rect.width() - 2 * textMargin;
+            const int required = viewItem->fontMetrics.horizontalAdvance(viewItem->text);
+            const int inset = qBound(0, available - required, leftInset + rightInset);
+
+            // Split what remains in the original proportion, so a reduced inset
+            // keeps the text positioned as it would have been.
+            const int total = leftInset + rightInset;
+            const int right = total > 0 ? inset * rightInset / total : 0;
+            rect.setRight(rect.right() - right);
+            rect.setLeft(rect.left() + (inset - right));
             rect.moveTop(rect.top() + margins.top() - margins.bottom());
         }
 
